WEBVTT
Kind: captions

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:12.000 position:63%
Ivan Aivazovsky is probably the man who painted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:15.440 position:63%
the sea better than anyone&nbsp;
else in the history of art.

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:22.080 position:63%
In his paintings we see storms tearing ships&nbsp;
apart, giant waves rising against the sky,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:28.080 position:63%
peaceful sunrises over the horizon, and nights&nbsp;
when the moonlight reflects on the calm water.

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:32.880 position:63%
His paintings are so impressive that, if&nbsp;
you look at them closely, the waves seem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:38.240 position:63%
transparent. Sunlight passes through them as&nbsp;
if they were made of glass, and inside them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:43.520 position:63%
we can see the green and blue of the water&nbsp;
shining as if they had a light of their own.

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:46.000 position:63%
But the most incredible thing of all is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:49.360 position:63%
almost none of these paintings&nbsp;
were made in front of the sea.

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:56.080 position:63%
Aivazovsky worked shut away in his studio. No&nbsp;
models, no outdoor sketches, and without even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.080 --> 00:01:01.680 position:63%
looking at the water. We could say that all&nbsp;
of his works came from his memory. And yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:06.720 position:63%
many consider him the man who painted the&nbsp;
sea better than anyone else in history.

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:14.000 position:63%
Ivan Aivazovsky was born in 1817 in Feodosia, a&nbsp;
port city in Crimea, on the shores of the Black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:19.760 position:63%
Sea. His family was of Armenian origin,&nbsp;
and they lived with very few resources.

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:23.920 position:63%
So few, in fact, that there were no&nbsp;
pencils or notebooks in his home. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:28.080 position:63%
is said that young Ivan learned to draw&nbsp;
on the walls of the houses in his town.

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:33.680 position:63%
And since he grew up in a coastal city, the sea&nbsp;
was part of his daily life. So it is not hard to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:39.360 position:63%
imagine that, from a very young age, it ended&nbsp;
up becoming the protagonist of his drawings.

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:44.560 position:63%
Over time, his talent drew so much attention&nbsp;
that, with the support of some influential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:49.280 position:63%
people in the region, he earned a place&nbsp;
at the Imperial Academy of Arts in Saint&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:55.440 position:63%
Petersburg. There, he quickly stood out, and he&nbsp;
even had the chance to meet the poet Alexander&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.440 --> 00:02:03.360 position:63%
Pushkin in 1836, during the writer’s visit to&nbsp;
the Academy. And the following year, in 1837,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:08.320 position:63%
he graduated with a gold medal,&nbsp;
two years earlier than expected.

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:13.280 position:63%
In 1840, the Academy sent him to&nbsp;
study in Europe. And it was there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:17.360 position:63%
that something happened that would&nbsp;
change his way of painting forever.

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:22.880 position:63%
Aivazovsky presented several paintings at an&nbsp;
exhibition. Some of them he had painted in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:28.080 position:63%
front of the landscape, copying what he saw,&nbsp;
while others he had painted in his studio,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:33.040 position:63%
entirely from memory. And to his surprise,&nbsp;
the public walked right past the ones he had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:37.840 position:63%
painted from life, and spent a long time&nbsp;
contemplating the ones he had invented.

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:42.560 position:63%
From that moment on, he understood something&nbsp;
he would defend for the rest of his life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:48.160 position:63%
As he himself once explained, a painter&nbsp;
who merely copies nature becomes its slave.

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:54.080 position:63%
The movements of living elements, like a flash of&nbsp;
lightning, a gust of wind, or the crash of a wave,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:59.680 position:63%
are impossible to capture with the brush, so the&nbsp;
artist must memorize them. And in his own words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:04.560 position:63%
the subject of his paintings took shape&nbsp;
in his memory, like that of a poet.

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:09.920 position:63%
In this way, Aivazovsky would often go&nbsp;
to the coast, observe the sea for hours,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:12.880 position:63%
and then return to his studio to paint it. That is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:16.800 position:63%
why in his works it is common to see a&nbsp;
storm he had witnessed years earlier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:21.840 position:63%
or the light of a particular sunrise that&nbsp;
had been etched into his mind forever.

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:25.600 position:63%
But now let us get to what made him unmistakable.

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:30.880 position:63%
If you look at any of his storms, you will see&nbsp;
that his waves are not dark masses of water. On&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:36.880 position:63%
the contrary, they are translucent. The light&nbsp;
enters from one side and comes out the other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:42.000 position:63%
illuminating the inside of the wave with&nbsp;
shades of green, turquoise, and gold.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:47.600 position:63%
To achieve this, Aivazovsky used a technique&nbsp;
called glazing, which consists of applying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:52.800 position:63%
very thin layers of paint, one on top of&nbsp;
another. Each layer lets through the light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:58.560 position:63%
of the one beneath it, and this creates that&nbsp;
sense of depth and transparency. This effect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:04.080 position:63%
became so characteristic that in Russia it&nbsp;
began to be called “the Aivazovsky wave.”

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:08.800 position:63%
His works were so difficult to imitate that&nbsp;
a rumor circulated among his colleagues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:12.960 position:63%
claiming that Aivazovsky bought his&nbsp;
paints from William Turner himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:18.560 position:63%
who was considered at the time the great English&nbsp;
master of light. Of course, what I am telling you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:24.080 position:63%
was only a rumor, but in a way it shows just&nbsp;
how much no one understood how he did it.

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:29.760 position:63%
And finally, there is one more detail that makes&nbsp;
his work even more impressive: the speed at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:36.400 position:63%
which he finished his paintings. Aivazovsky could&nbsp;
complete enormous canvases in a matter of hours.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:42.080 position:63%
In fact, his work The Creation of the World,&nbsp;
almost two and a half meters wide, is known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:48.160 position:63%
to have been painted in a single day. Nine hours&nbsp;
were all he needed to paint such a masterpiece.

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:48.560 position:63%
La Novena Ola

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:52.480 position:63%
Now, in 1850, at just thirty-three years old,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:57.600 position:63%
Aivazovsky painted the work that would&nbsp;
turn him into a legend: The Ninth Wave.

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:00.800 position:63%
The title comes from an old sailors’ superstition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:06.640 position:63%
which held that during a storm, the ninth wave&nbsp;
was always the biggest and the most dangerous.

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:12.080 position:63%
In the painting, we see a group of shipwrecked&nbsp;
men clinging to the remains of a mast, in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:18.320 position:63%
middle of a raging sea, while a gigantic wave is&nbsp;
about to crash down on them. But behind the storm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:23.600 position:63%
the sun begins to break through the clouds,&nbsp;
and its golden light illuminates the water.

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:26.880 position:63%
It is a scene of destruction, but also of hope.

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:32.560 position:63%
Tsar Nicholas I bought the painting that same&nbsp;
year. And today it is held at the State Russian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:38.320 position:63%
Museum in Saint Petersburg, where it remains&nbsp;
one of the most admired works in the collection.

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:41.040 position:63%
But his fame was not limited to Russia.

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:42.560 position:63%
During his travels through Europe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:46.880 position:63%
the Italian press went as far as to&nbsp;
write that Aivazovsky depicted light,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:54.080 position:63%
air, and water more convincingly than any painter&nbsp;
before him. In 1843, he exhibited at the Louvre,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:59.280 position:63%
in an international exhibition where he was the&nbsp;
only representative from Russia, and he received&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:05.840 position:63%
a gold medal. Years later, in 1857, France&nbsp;
made him a knight of the Legion of Honour.

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:11.280 position:63%
And his prestige has never stopped&nbsp;
growing. In 2012, one of his paintings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:15.600 position:63%
The Survivors, sold at Sotheby’s&nbsp;
for more than four million pounds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:19.600 position:63%
becoming one of the most expensive&nbsp;
Russian works ever auctioned.

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:25.840 position:63%
Aivazovsky lived a long life full of recognition.&nbsp;
He exhibited his works throughout Europe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:30.880 position:63%
traveled across the Mediterranean, and even&nbsp;
crossed the Atlantic to the United States,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:35.600 position:63%
where he saw Niagara Falls, which he&nbsp;
would later also paint from memory.

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:41.280 position:63%
But he never stopped returning to Feodosia.&nbsp;
There, he founded an art school, a library,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:46.560 position:63%
and a painting gallery, and became the great&nbsp;
benefactor of the city where he was born.

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:52.640 position:63%
Something truly impressive about his career&nbsp;
is that his energy never faded. In 1898,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:56.080 position:63%
at eighty-one years old,&nbsp;
he painted Among the Waves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.080 --> 00:07:01.440 position:63%
a dark and violent storm that many consider&nbsp;
one of his most extraordinary works.

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:07.120 position:63%
He finally passed away in Feodosia&nbsp;
in 1900, at the age of eighty-two,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:10.960 position:63%
facing the same sea he had&nbsp;
seen since he was a child.

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:15.840 position:63%
An art critic once said that Aivazovsky&nbsp;
should not be called a marine painter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:20.080 position:63%
but a painter of the elements. And&nbsp;
perhaps he was right. Because what he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:25.280 position:63%
painted was not only the water, but its&nbsp;
strength, its light, and its movement.

00:07:25.280 --> 00:07:27.680 position:63%
I would love to know which&nbsp;
of his paintings you find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:30.880 position:63%
the most impressive. Let me know in the comments.

